# Copy this file to ".env" and fill in real values to enable outgoing
# email (account verification + password reset). server.js loads .env
# automatically. WITHOUT these, the app still works in dev — the codes
# are printed to the server console instead of being emailed.
#
# ── PREFERRED: Brevo HTTP API ────────────────────────────────────────
# Sends over HTTPS (port 443), so it works on hosts that block outbound
# SMTP ports — notably Render's free tier blocks ports 25/465/587, which
# breaks the raw-SMTP transport below. When BREVO_API_KEY is set, it is
# used instead of SMTP. The sender identity still comes from SMTP_FROM,
# which must be an address verified in Brevo (a domain is NOT required —
# Brevo lets you verify a single sender address like your GMX mailbox).
#
# One-time setup:
#   1. Create a free account at https://www.brevo.com (300 emails/day).
#   2. Senders & IP → Senders → Add a sender → enter your GMX address
#      (e.g. pixelparties@gmx.de). Brevo emails it a confirmation link;
#      click it. The sender is now verified.
#   3. SMTP & API → API Keys → Generate a new API key → put it in
#      BREVO_API_KEY (below). On Render, set it as an environment var.
#   4. Set SMTP_FROM to the verified sender (name + that address).
# BREVO_API_KEY=xkeysib-...
#
# ── ALTERNATIVE: direct SMTP (blocked on Render free tier) ───────────
# ── GMX free mailbox, direct SMTP ────────────────────────────────────
# Sends verification mail straight through GMX's SMTP. Free, no domain
# needed. The From address MUST be the GMX account itself — GMX will not
# let you send as any other address.
#
# One-time setup:
#   1. Create a free mailbox at https://www.gmx.net (or gmx.com), e.g.
#      pixelparties@gmx.de
#   2. In GMX webmail: Settings → POP3/IMAP → ENABLE "POP3 and IMAP access".
#      External SMTP is blocked until this is on.
#   3. Put the full address in SMTP_USER and SMTP_FROM, and your GMX
#      account password in SMTP_PASS.
#   4. Host: use mail.gmx.net for gmx.de/.net accounts, mail.gmx.com for
#      gmx.com accounts (mail.gmx.com generally works for all).
#
# Note: GMX free has daily send limits and weaker transactional
# deliverability — if codes land in spam, switch to a relay (see git
# history / Brevo) or a real domain.
SMTP_HOST=mail.gmx.net
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=pixelparties@gmx.de
SMTP_PASS=your-gmx-account-password
SMTP_FROM=Pixel Parties <pixelparties@gmx.de>
# SMTP_SECURE is inferred from the port (587 = STARTTLS, 465 = implicit TLS).
# GMX supports both; override only if you switch to 465:
# SMTP_SECURE=true
#
# If a TLS-intercepting antivirus/proxy (e.g. Avast Mail Shield) on the
# host re-signs the SMTP connection, Node rejects the cert with
# "self-signed certificate in certificate chain". Preferred fix: disable
# the antivirus's SSL/mail scanning. Local-dev fallback only (never on a
# public server) — skip cert verification:
# SMTP_TLS_INSECURE=true

# ── "Sign in with Google" (optional) ─────────────────────────────────
# Enables the Google button on the login screen. Only the PUBLIC client id
# is needed (no client secret) — it's safe to expose to the browser. When
# unset, the button simply doesn't appear.
#
# One-time setup (Google Cloud Console, free, no billing):
#   1. console.cloud.google.com → create/select a project.
#   2. APIs & Services → OAuth consent screen → External → fill app name +
#      support email; add yourself as a Test user (or Publish to go live).
#   3. APIs & Services → Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID
#      → Application type: Web application.
#   4. Authorized JavaScript origins → add each origin the app runs on:
#        https://pixelparties.onrender.com
#        http://localhost:3000   (use your local port)
#      (No redirect URI is needed — this uses the GIS ID-token flow.)
#   5. Copy the generated Client ID (ends in .apps.googleusercontent.com)
#      into GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID. On Render, set it as an environment variable.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=xxxxxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
